---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How we do this"
  description="How Gridwatch turns public electricity files into a daily website."
>
  <article>
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>How we do this</h1>
      <p>
        The numbers on this site come from files the Electricity Authority
        publishes. The Authority is the independent public body that regulates
        New Zealand's electricity market and sets its rules. Transpower is the
        system operator, the organisation that keeps the high-voltage network
        working from minute to minute. This site is separate from both.
      </p>
      <p>
        The files sit on a catalogue the Authority calls Electricity Market
        Information, or EMI, and on a public file store behind that catalogue.
        Gridwatch downloads the files from that store.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <figcaption>What Gridwatch downloads</figcaption>
      <p>
        Seven kinds of file, all from that store. Six of them feed the daily
        pages. Cleared offers are downloaded and tidied, then left for later.
      </p>
      <div class="source-groups">
        <section>
          <h2>Used for the daily pages</h2>
          <ul class="sources">
            <li>
              <strong>Generation output</strong>
              <p>
                Metered energy from each plant, in kilowatt-hours per half hour.
                One file covers a calendar month. Gridwatch uses this file for
                generation once it is out, usually a few weeks after the day.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nodal prices and volumes</strong>
              <p>
                A node is a named place on the grid. This file has load and
                generation at each node, in five-minute slices. Recent daily
                pages use those generation figures until the monthly meter file
                is published. The pages use dispatch energy prices for the
                price at a place, not this file.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Dispatch energy prices</strong>
              <p>
                The wholesale price at each named place on the grid, in dollars
                per megawatt-hour. Dispatch is the market's word for the prices
                set as the grid is run. One megawatt-hour is 1,000
                kilowatt-hours, the same kWh unit as a line on a bill. One file
                per trading day, which is a calendar day in New Zealand time.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Dispatch reserve prices</strong>
              <p>
                Reserve is spare generating capacity, held in case a plant
                trips. These files are the prices paid for that spare capacity
                as the grid is run. The daily page shows each day's lowest,
                average and highest reserve price for each island, for fast
                reserve (which acts within 6 seconds) and slow reserve (within
                60 seconds).
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Generation fleet</strong>
              <p>
                Which plants exist, what they burn, and who runs them. Gridwatch
                uses this to group generation by fuel.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Network supply points</strong>
              <p>
                Where local networks connect to the grid. Gridwatch uses this to
                put a plant on the North Island or the South Island, and, where
                the files include coordinates, to place a named point on the
                wholesale-price map.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Downloaded, not on the pages yet</h2>
          <ul class="sources">
            <li>
              <strong>Cleared offers</strong>
              <p>
                How much each plant was accepted to generate or to hold as
                reserve. Cleared means the market took that part of the plant's
                offer. Downloaded and tidied, not shown yet.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What happens to the files</h2>
      <p>
        After a trading day finishes, Gridwatch downloads yesterday's files,
        tidies them, and builds ordinary web pages from them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work has three stages, labelled bronze, silver, and gold. The
        names say how far each file is through the process.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <figcaption>From an EMI file to a page</figcaption>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li class="pipeline-step layer-bronze">
          <span class="layer-chip">Bronze</span>
          <strong>Download</strong>
          <p>
            Fetch each file from the Authority's store and keep it unchanged.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step layer-silver">
          <span class="layer-chip">Silver</span>
          <strong>Tidy</strong>
          <p>
            Check the file and line up dates and names, including when the
            clocks change. Join the plant list so later steps know fuel and
            island.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step layer-gold">
          <span class="layer-chip">Gold</span>
          <strong>Summarise</strong>
          <p>
            Build generation by fuel and plant, and prices at Ōtāhuhu, Haywards,
            and Benmore.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="pipeline-step">
          <span class="layer-chip layer-chip-site">Site</span>
          <strong>Publish</strong>
          <p>
            Write one file per trading day for the website (gold/site), then
            turn those files into ordinary web pages.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </figure>

    <div class="layers">
      <section class="layer layer-bronze">
        <span class="layer-chip">Bronze</span>
        <h2>The original download</h2>
        <p>
          Bronze is the file exactly as the Authority published it, plus a
          download log that records which version arrived. Gridwatch doesn't
          edit these files. If the Authority overwrites a day later, the old
          copy stays as well as the new one.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="layer layer-silver">
        <span class="layer-chip">Silver</span>
        <h2>The same records, made consistent</h2>
        <p>
          Silver is bronze after a check: dates and names lined up, with the
          original codes kept. A day whose file fails the check is left out
          rather than guessed. A day that is only partly there is kept, with the
          missing half-hours marked.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="layer layer-gold">
        <span class="layer-chip">Gold</span>
        <h2>The numbers on the site</h2>
        <p>
          Gold is built from silver, not from the original spreadsheets. The
          website reads only the gold files made for it: one per trading day,
          in a folder called gold/site, plus a short index of which days exist.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Once a day, after the Authority has put yesterday's files up (usually
        the next morning), Gridwatch rebuilds the pages from those gold files.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="prose" id="ai" aria-labelledby="ai-heading">
      <h2 id="ai-heading">How this site uses AI</h2>
      <p>
        A large language model, a kind of artificial intelligence (AI), writes
        three kinds of passage: the short write-up under each day's headline,
        which also leads the home page, the "In the last 7 days" paragraph on the home
        page, and the longer write-ups of unusual stretches. Each one
        carries a "Written by AI" label and lists the figures it was written
        from.
      </p>
      <p>
        The model gets the figures for that day, week or stretch and nothing
        else: for a day, the moments this site picked out, the day's totals and
        the half-hour prices they point to; for a week, a few figures from each
        day; for a stretch, its figures labelled by how sure each one is. It doesn't search the web. Its instructions
        say to add no fact beyond those figures, and to hedge any cause that
        isn't confirmed.
      </p>
      <p>
        A program then checks every number in the write-up against those
        figures. If a number doesn't match, the program logs a warning and the
        write-up is still published. A day or week write-up longer than two
        sentences isn't published, and the page shows its usual summary instead. Nobody
        reviews the write-ups by hand, so read any "why" in them as a likely
        explanation, not a finding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything else on this site comes from fixed rules, not AI: the map,
        the charts, the moments in each day, the headlines and the tables.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  article {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .source-groups {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .source-groups h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources li,
  .pipeline-step,
  .layer {
    background: var(--color-card);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    hyphens: none;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .sources strong,
  .pipeline-step strong {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .sources p,
  .pipeline-step p {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .pipeline {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .pipeline-step,
  .layer {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-brand);
  }

  .layer-bronze {
    border-left-color: var(--color-layer-bronze);
  }

  .layer-silver {
    border-left-color: var(--color-layer-silver);
  }

  .layer-gold {
    border-left-color: var(--color-layer-gold);
  }

  .layer-chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    align-self: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    background: var(--color-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .layer-chip::before {
    content: "";
    width: 0.5rem;
    height: 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-brand);
  }

  .layer-bronze > .layer-chip {
    border-color: var(--color-layer-bronze);
  }

  .layer-bronze > .layer-chip::before {
    background: var(--color-layer-bronze);
  }

  .layer-silver > .layer-chip {
    border-color: var(--color-layer-silver);
  }

  .layer-silver > .layer-chip::before {
    background: var(--color-layer-silver);
  }

  .layer-gold > .layer-chip {
    border-color: var(--color-layer-gold);
  }

  .layer-gold > .layer-chip::before {
    background: var(--color-layer-gold);
  }

  .layer-chip-site::before {
    background: var(--color-brand);
  }

  .layers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .source-groups {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-5);
    }

    .pipeline {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .layers {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 70rem) {
    .pipeline {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
